<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lu Xiufu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1236">1236</a>–<a href="/years/1279">1279</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lu Xiufu (1236–1279) was a Chinese official of the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> and one of its last loyalist leaders. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took the capital Lin'an in 1276, he and the general Zhang Shijie led the remnant court in flight along the southern coast. When the Song fleet was destroyed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamen">Battle of Yamen</a> in 1279, he leapt into the sea carrying the boy emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-bing-of-song">Zhao Bing</a>, ending the dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lu-xiufu.jpg" alt="Lu Xiufu" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qing Gu Yuanji · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%99%B8%E7%A7%80%E5%A4%AB.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lu Xiufu, courtesy name Junshi, was born on 8 November 1236 and came from Yancheng, present-day Jianhu County in Jiangsu Province. In 1256 he passed the imperial examination in the same year as Wen Tianxiang, becoming a presented scholar, and he then served in the Ministry of Rites as a vice-minister.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 18 January 1276 the Yuan general Bayan arrived with his army outside Lin'an. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> court sent Lu to negotiate peace, but he was forced to surrender and was then released. After the capital fell and the five-year-old Emperor Gong was taken prisoner, Lu, with Chen Yizhong, Zhang Shijie and Consort Yang, looked after the two sons of Emperor Duzong, Zhao Shi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-bing-of-song">Zhao Bing</a>. Later that year Zhao Shi was enthroned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> as Emperor Duanzong, and he made Lu military adviser to the Privy Council, charged with continuing the resistance.</p><p>When Duanzong died at the age of ten in 1278, Lu and Zhang Shijie enthroned his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-bing-of-song">Zhao Bing</a>, while Consort Yang, now Empress Dowager, ran the court from behind a screen. Lu became Left Chancellor and governed with Zhang Shijie.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1279 Yuan forces under Zhang Hongfan attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> at Mount Ya, present-day Yamen, and in the battle of 19 March 1279 the Song army and navy were wiped out. Unwilling to be captured, Lu first ordered his younger wife to kill herself, then urged the boy emperor not to share the shame of Emperor Gong, held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>. He gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-bing-of-song">Zhao Bing</a> his seal, took him in his arms and jumped from a cliff into the sea. Many concubines and ministers also died, and by July tens of thousands of corpses were floating in the sea.</p><p>With Wen Tianxiang and Zhang Shijie, Lu is regarded as one of the Three Loyal Princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>. A memorial hall to them stands in Jiangmen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, and the Three Loyalists Temple, built in 1868 under the Tongzhi Emperor, stands in the Shuangxi District of New Taipei City in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. His descendants settled in Qiangang Village in Conghua, Guangdong.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lu Xiufu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A loyalist fleet of more than a thousand ships was destroyed, and the minister <b>Lu Xiufu</b> leapt into the sea carrying the last Song emperor, the seven-year-old Zhao Bing; many officials followed them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Battle of Yamen on the Pearl River Delta in 1279 the Yuan army under Zhang Hongfan crushed the last resistance; the 7-year-old emperor Zhao Bing committed suicide with Prime Minister <b>Lu Xiufu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The minister <b>Lu Xiufu</b> jumped into the sea with the child emperor, completing Kublai Khan's conquest of China.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-19">19 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yancheng, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.35,120.16&amp;year=1263" aria-label="Yancheng on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7622 953 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lu Xiufu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8072 1223h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8072 1223h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.35,120.16&amp;year=1263">What was here in 1263? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
